\label{sec:summary}
\section{Summary}

Long-term exposure to ambient PM$_{2.5}$ is associated with increased all-cause mortality \autocite{pope_fine_2020}. Wildfire is a significant and growing source of PM$_{2.5}$ exposure, with
climate change projected to amplify fire activity and smoke concentrations across many regions \autocite{pierce_estimating_2017, zhao_global_2025}. Yet the mortality damages from warming-induced fire PM$_{2.5}$ are absent from existing global estimates of the social cost of carbon dioxide (SC-CO$_2$).

The GIVE integrated assessment model estimates the SC-CO$_2$ with four damage sectors---temperature-related mortality, agriculture, energy, and sea-level rise \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}---but omits fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality as a pathway for warming damages. Recent work by \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} estimates a partial SC-CO$_2$ accounting for fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality in the United States, finding it to be a quantitatively important damage component. No global estimate yet exists.

In this study, we develop a global damage function linking global mean temperature (GMT) change to fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality, which we incorporate as a fifth damage sector in GIVE. We estimate country-level relationships between GMT change and population-weighted fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure---$\beta_c$ from Equation \eqref{eq:regression}---using a fixed-effects regression pooling data from multiple global fire model simulations, and use these estimates alongside a concentration-response coefficient to compute monetized mortality damages and a global SC-CO$_2$ (Section \ref{sec:method}).

We additionally estimate the GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship at the grid-cell level to characterize where warming most strongly amplifies fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure, $\beta_i$. Following \textcite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}, we assess uncertainty in our SC-CO$_2$ estimates via Monte Carlo simulation, sampling over both the concentration-response coefficient and the country-level GMT-exposure coefficients (Section \ref{sec:method_uncertainty}). We discuss the strengths and limitations of our approach relative to \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} (Section \ref{sec:app_qiu_method}) and compare our U.S. partial SC-CO$_2$ estimate to theirs (Section \ref{sec:app_usa}).

