\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present results in two parts. First, we map grid-cell-level $\beta_i$ estimates---the estimated change in fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentration ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) per 1\textdegree C increase in GMT at each grid cell---to characterize where warming most strongly amplifies fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure (Figure~\ref{fig:beta_grid_map}). Second, we report the global SC-CO$_2$ attributable to fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality using our GIVE fire PM$_{2.5}$ damage function (Figure~\ref{fig:global_results_scco2}). This function uses country-level $\beta_c$ estimates---the change in population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) per 1\textdegree C increase in GMT for country $c$---from our regression (Figure~\ref{fig:beta_hist_map}; Section~\ref{sec:method_regression}). 

In Figure~\ref{fig:beta_grid_map}, the highest $\beta_i$ values are concentrated in boreal North America, boreal Asia, the Congo Basin, and the Amazon basin---regions\footnote{Large parts of Russia and Canada exhibit the most intense responses, consistent with recent evidence of surging fire activity across high-latitude boreal forests driven by warming and increasing drought \autocite{zhao_global_2025}. Fire activity in the Congo Basin tropical forests has also increased, with year-to-year variation synchronized with rising temperatures and vapor pressure deficit, alongside deforestation \autocite{wimberly_increasing_2024}.} where climate change has been shown to increase fire activity through rising temperatures and drying of fuel loads \autocite{park_attributing_2024, zhao_global_2025, wimberly_increasing_2024}. Moderate positive $\beta_i$ is broadly distributed across temperate North America, Europe, and parts of South and Southeast Asia. A minority of cells show near-zero or negative $\beta_i$, concentrated in parts of sub-Saharan Africa and Australia. In sub-Saharan Africa, there is evidence that competing mechanisms\footnote{Higher temperatures may increase ignition potential while also driving evapotranspiration that may reduce grass productivity and fuel loads \autocite{park_attributing_2024}.} may offset one another as well as disagreement among fire-vegetation models on the impact of climate change in this region \autocite{park_attributing_2024}. Australia shows unexpectedly flat or negative $\beta_i$, driven by flat or declining predicted fire PM$_{2.5}$ in the input data from \textcite{zhao_global_2025} and \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} (Figure \ref{fig:lof}).

Figure~\ref{fig:global_results_scco2} reports the global SC-CO$_2$ attributable to fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality, alongside contributions from existing GIVE damage sectors. Adding to existing uncertainty captured in GIVE, we incorporate conditional uncertainty in the fire PM$_{2.5}$ damage function parameters (Section~\ref{sec:method_uncertainty}). The fire PM$_{2.5}$ SC-CO$_2$ has a mean of \$47/ton CO$_2$ (95\% CI: \$10.2--\$102.1) at a 2\% discount rate (Panel A). Panel B places the fire PM$_{2.5}$ estimate in context: at \$47/ton CO$_2$, fire PM$_{2.5}$ represents the third-largest damage sector after temperature mortality (\$96.1) and agriculture (\$99.8), and increases the total global SC-CO$_2$ from {\color{red}\$208 to \$255}---an increase of 23\%. A direct comparison of our U.S. partial SC-CO$_2$ with \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} is reported in Section~\ref{sec:app_usa}. Sectoral and total SC-CO$_2$ estimates exhibit substantial regional heterogeneity, as reported in Table~\ref{tab:scco2_region_n100}.

The SC-CO$_2$ results are driven by the \emph{marginal} excess death associated with fire PM$_{2.5}$, computed by subtracting projected excess death in a baseline model from the same projection with one additional ton of CO$_2$, where excess death is a function of GMT change (Equation \eqref{eq:damage}). Globally, warming induces an average of 264,844 excess deaths per year at 1.5$^\circ$C, rising to 528,136 at 3$^\circ$C and 651,380 at 4$^\circ$C. We compare our U.S.\ excess death estimates and damage function shape to \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} in Section~\ref{sec:app_usa}: relative to the \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} quadratic, our linear function projects more deaths at low warming levels and less deaths at high warming levels.

