\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We estimate that incorporating fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality as a fifth damage sector in the GIVE integrated assessment model increases the global SC-CO$_2$ by 23\%, from \$208 to \$255 per ton CO$_2$ at a 2\% discount
rate. At \$47/ton CO$_2$, fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality is the third-largest damage sector in GIVE, after temperature mortality and agriculture, and represents a quantitatively important omission from existing global
SC-CO$_2$ estimates. These results extend \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}, who estimate a partial U.S.\ SC-CO$_2$ of \$11.2 from fire PM$_{2.5}$ mortality, to a global estimate. The magnitude of the global fire
PM$_{2.5}$ damage component---and its substantial regional heterogeneity (Table~\ref{tab:scco2_region_n100})---suggests that omitting this pathway from integrated assessment models likely understates the societal cost of greenhouse gas emissions and the benefits of climate mitigation.

Our estimates are subject to several limitations. The concentration-response coefficient $\gamma$ is drawn from a meta-analysis of long-term all-source PM$_{2.5}$ exposure in Europe, North America, and Asia \autocite{pope_fine_2020}, and is applied uniformly across all countries, abstracting from regional heterogeneity in population susceptibility and background pollution levels. The GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ slope $\beta_c$ is identified from 25 observations per country spanning a small number of fire models and warming levels. This allows us to estimate a linear relationship between GMT and PM$_{2.5}$ exposure, but it does not allow us to test for curvature. Our Monte Carlo intervals are conditional on the linear functional form, the set of source studies, and the assumption that fire-model fixed effects absorb cross-model differences in levels rather than slopes. Our analysis cannot capture the full structural uncertainty in the GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship (Section~\ref{sec:method_uncertainty}). {\color{red}Need to speak to separability of temp-related death and fire pm death, see ``separable'' in \autocite{qiu_valuing_2026}} We discuss these limitations in detail in Sections \ref{sec:method_uncertainty} and ~\ref{sec:app_qiu_method}

A key design feature of our approach is reliance on process-based Earth System Model (ESM) output to characterize the GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship (Section~\ref{sec:method_regression}). Unlike empirical climate-fire models, the ESMs underlying \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}, \textcite{park_attributing_2024}, and \textcite{zhao_global_2025} explicitly represent physical mechanisms linking warming to fire activity---including dynamic vegetation response, carbon and nitrogen biogeochemistry, and changes in fuel load---that are not captured by statistical approaches trained on historical observations alone. These feedbacks between fire activity, vegetation, and future fuel availability are embedded in the simulation output from which we estimate $\beta_c$, so our damage function implicitly incorporates their influence on the GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship without requiring a separate empirical correction. \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}, by contrast, estimate and apply an empirical fire-fuel feedback to their projections, and acknowledge that their framework does not capture climate's influence on vegetation productivity or lightning-related ignitions \autocite[942]{qiu_wildfire_2025}. This structural advantage of process-based modeling is one reason we view our global damage function as a defensible complement to the U.S.-focused empirical approach of \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}, despite the more limited data available for our regression.

