\section{Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{GMT change and fire PM2.5 exposure}
\label{sec:method_regression}
To relate fire-attributable PM$_{2.5}$ exposure to global mean temperature (GMT) change,\footnote{We interpret GMT as a policy-relevant index of global climate change rather than as the direct physical driver of fire PM$_{2.5}$. Fire activity and smoke exposure respond mechanistically to regional temperature, vapor pressure deficit, precipitation, fuel availability, vegetation, ignition, suppression, land use, and atmospheric transport \autocite{pierce_estimating_2017, park_attributing_2024, zhao_global_2025, wimberly_increasing_2024}. Therefore, $\beta_c$ is not ``the effect of GMT'' in a mechanistic sense. It is a reduced-form mapping from global warming level to the fire-PM$_{2.5}$ exposure implied by the available model ensemble. This parameterization is appropriate given the limited availability of globally harmonized fire-PM$_{2.5}$ projections and allows us to construct a country-level damage-function parameter for GIVE, where GMT is the state variable linking emissions to climate damages.} we estimate a country-level fixed effects regression for each country $c$:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:regression}
\bar{PM}_{ctsm}
=
\beta_c T_{ts}
+
\alpha_{cm}
+
\varepsilon_{ctsm}, 
\end{align}
where $\bar{PM}_{ctsm}$ is population-weighted\footnote{See Section \ref{sec:app_pop_weight} for population-weighting detail.} per-capita fire PM${2.5}$ exposure ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) for country $c$, time period $t$, climate scenario $s$, and fire model $m$; $T_{ts}$ is the GMT anomaly relative to the 1850-1900 pre-industrial baseline ($^\circ$C); $\alpha_{cm}$ is a country-fire-model-specific intercept; and $\varepsilon_{ctsm}$ is a residual. The coefficient of interest, $\beta_c$, captures the change in population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure associated with a 1°C increase in GMT for country $c$.  We assume that this relationship is linear and constant over time\footnote{\textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} estimate a quadratic damage function relating GMST to wildfire-smoke mortality rates in the United States. A more flexible functional form is appropriate in their setting because their analysis is U.S.-specific and uses a much richer simulation design: they combine an empirically estimated North American climate-fire-smoke model with projections from 28 CMIP6 climate models, yielding 784 climate simulations spanning approximately 0.95--6.93$^\circ$C of warming. By contrast, our objective is to estimate country-specific GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure relationships globally using harmonized output from a small number of heterogeneous fire-PM modeling studies. For most countries, this provides relatively few distinct warming levels and limited within-model variation with which to identify curvature. We therefore impose a linear specification as a parsimonious approximation to the GMT--fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship. This avoids fitting curvature that is weakly identified by our data and avoids imposing stronger assumptions about high-warming extrapolation. In this context, the linear specification is also conservative relative to convex specifications.}.

We estimate equation~\eqref{eq:regression} at two spatial scales. At the country level, the dependent variable is population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure $\bar{PM}_{ctsm}$, yielding the country-specific coefficient $\beta_c$ that enters the GIVE damage function. At the grid-cell level, the dependent variable is grid-cell fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure $PM_{itsm}$, yielding a grid-cell-specific coefficient $\beta_i$---the estimated change in fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentration ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) per 1\textdegree C increase in GMT at grid cell $i$---used solely to characterize the spatial pattern of GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ sensitivity (Figure~\ref{fig:beta_grid_map}).

The dependent variable is constructed by combining three sources of gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ data, each paired with its corresponding GMT value. \textcite{park_attributing_2024} provide historical decadal mean fire PM$_{2.5}$ for the 1960s through 2010s under three fire models (CLASSIC, JULES, SSiB4), yielding 18 observations per country (3 models $\times$ 6 decades). \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} provide projections for a baseline period (2001–2010) and two future periods (2041-2050, 2091-2100) under RCP4.5 and RCP8.5 using the CESM fire model, contributing 5 observations. \textcite{zhao_global_2025} provide two additional projections for 2095-2099 under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, for a total of 25 observations per country. These gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure data are aggregated to population-weighted per-capita country-level exposures.

The five fire models differ systematically in their absolute estimates of PM$_{2.5}$ exposure due to differences in model structure rather than the GMT-fire relationship of interest in this paper. Without accounting for these differences, we would misattribute to GMT the variation in exposure levels driven by fire models. We address this by including fire-model fixed effects $\alpha_{cm}$, which absorb model-specific mean exposure differences so that $\beta_c$ is identified from within-model covariation between GMT and fire PM$_{2.5}$ across periods and scenarios\footnote{Figure \ref{fig:lof} in the Appendix illustrates this for select countries.}. This specification estimates the average slope $\beta_c$ within all five fire models. If fire models disagree on the slope, the regression standard errors reflect this disagreement only indirectly---as residual variance around the pooled slope---rather than isolating between-model slope variation as a distinct uncertainty component (Section \ref{sec:method_uncertainty}).

The resulting $\beta_c$ estimate is used in the GIVE integrated assessment model as the country-level damage function parameter linking GMT change to fire PM${2.5}$ exposure and its associated mortality. Across 174 countries, $\beta_c$ is positive for 86\% of countries (median = 0.18 $\mu$g/m$^3$/yr per $^\circ$C), indicating that warming broadly increases population exposure to fire PM$_{2.5}$. Estimates are statistically significant at the 5\% level for 60\% of countries. Spatially, the largest responses are concentrated in northern North and South America, northern Asia, central Africa, and parts of Southeast Asia, while a minority of countries---predominantly in sub-Saharan Africa as well as Australia---exhibit negative $\beta_c$ (Figure \ref{fig:beta_hist_map}). 

