\subsection{PM2.5 exposure and mortality}
Defining a fire PM$_{2.5}$ damage function in GIVE also requires a parameter relating PM$_{2.5}$ exposure and mortality. Following \textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} and \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}
\footnote{These studies use the all-source PM2.5 \textcite{pope_fine_2020} result for exposure levels outside historical observations in the U.S., those $>6$ $\mu$g/m$^3$, while using their own fire PM2.5-mortality response estimate for exposure $<6$ $\mu$g/m$^3$. We instead apply the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} result for all exposure levels. Since there is evidence of greater toxicity from fire PM2.5 \autocite{qiu_wildfire_2025}, we believe our estimated impact to be conservative. 
}, we source this parameter from the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} meta-analysis of 33 selected studies in Europe, North America, and Asia of long-term exposure to PM$_{2.5}$. Using a random effects model, they estimate a hazard ratio (HR) of 1.08 (95\% CI =1.06-1.11) for all-cause mortality per 10~$\mu$g/m$^3$. This estimate is a bit lower than the \textcite{orellano_long-term_2024} meta-analysis, which reports a central estimate of 1.095 (95\%~CI: 1.064--1.127) per 10~$\mu$g/m$^3$. 

We use the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} estimate to calculate the percent increase in all-cause mortality rates per $\mu$g/m$^3$ as $ \gamma = \frac{1.08-1}{10}
= 0.008$. This 0.8\% increase per $\mu$g/m$^3$ is applied to all countries globally. 

\subsection{GIVE fire PM2.5 Damage Function}
Our GIVE damage function takes a linear form analogous to the existing temperature-related mortality module in \textcite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}. We translate warming-induced fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure into monetized mortality damages similarly. For country $c$ at time $t$, monetized damages are
\begin{align}
\label{eq:damage}
C_{ct} = V_{ct} \cdot M_{ct} \cdot \gamma \cdot \beta_c \cdot T_t,
\end{align}
where $V_{ct}$ is the value of a statistical life (VSL) in USD, $M_{ct}$ is the baseline all-cause mortality count, $\gamma = 0.008$ is the concentration-response coefficient (the proportional increase in mortality risk per 1~$\mu$g/m$^3$ increase in long-term PM$_{2.5}$ exposure), $\beta_c$ is the country-specific regression coefficient estimated in equation~\eqref{eq:regression} (the change in population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure per 1\textdegree C increase in GMT), and $T_t$ is the GMT anomaly relative to the pre-industrial baseline (\textdegree C).

The product $M_{ct} \cdot \gamma \cdot \beta_c \cdot T_t$ gives the number of excess deaths attributable to warming-induced fire PM$_{2.5}$ in country $c$ at time $t$. Multiplying by $V_{ct}$ converts these excess deaths to monetary damages. The VSL is sourced from \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}: a baseline U.S. VSL for 2020 is adjusted for each country and year using GDP per capita with an income elasticity of one. The baseline all-cause mortality count, $M_{ct}$, is likewise sourced from \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}, which projects these values for country and year from 2025 to 2300. 

\subsection{Uncertainty}
\label{sec:method_uncertainty}

Following \textcite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}, we assess uncertainty in our SC-CO$_2$ estimates via Monte Carlo simulation. In GIVE, each simulation draws from uncertain socioeconomic and emissions trajectories (RFF-SPs), climate model parameters (FaIR), sea-level model parameters (BRICK), and damage function coefficients \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}. To the damage function uncertainty already present in GIVE, we add sampling over the two parameters specific to our fire PM$_{2.5}$ component: the concentration-response coefficient $\gamma$ and the country-level GMT-exposure coefficient $\beta_c$. 

For $\gamma$, we draw from a normal distribution with mean $\bar{\gamma} = 0.008$ and standard error $SE(\gamma)= 0.00128$ derived from the 95\% confidence interval of the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} hazard ratio (HR $= 1.08$; 95\%~CI: $1.06$--$1.11$ per 10~$\mu$g/m$^3$), converted to
per-unit exposure as $\gamma = (\text{HR} - 1)/10$ \footnote{We have: 
$    SE(\gamma)
    = \frac{\gamma_{\text{upper}} - \gamma_{\text{lower}}}{2 \times 1.96}
    = \frac{0.011 - 0.006}{3.92}
    \approx 0.00128
$. 
}. For $\beta_c$, we draw independently for each country from a normal distribution with mean $\hat{\beta}_c$ and standard error $\widehat{SE}(\hat{\beta}_c)$ from the country-level fixed-effects regression in equation~\eqref{eq:regression}.

Several sources of uncertainty are not captured by this approach. We apply the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} concentration-response estimate globally, abstracting from regional heterogeneity in population susceptibility and background pollution levels. As noted in Section~\ref{sec:method_regression}, the regression standard errors underlying our $\beta_c$ draws reflect between-model slope disagreement only indirectly---as residual variance around the pooled slope---rather than isolating it as a distinct uncertainty component; the Monte Carlo confidence intervals therefore do not decompose how much $\beta_c$ would differ under an alternative fire model. Finally, we treat the linear functional form linking GMT to fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure as known, so uncertainty about potential nonlinearities in this relationship is not reflected in our confidence intervals. %These omissions likely cause us to understate the true uncertainty in our SC-CO$_2$ estimates. 

These intervals should therefore be interpreted as conditional uncertainty intervals rather than full structural uncertainty bounds. They reflect uncertainty in the concentration-response coefficient and sampling uncertainty in the estimated country-level GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ coefficients, conditional on the set of source studies used, our harmonization procedure, the linear GMT-exposure specification, the assumption that model fixed effects absorb cross-model differences in levels rather than slopes, and the global transferability of the PM$_{2.5}$ mortality coefficient. They do not fully capture uncertainty from alternative fire models, alternative functional forms, regional heterogeneity in smoke toxicity or population vulnerability, future adaptation, or changes in within-country population exposure. As a result, the reported intervals likely understate the full uncertainty surrounding the fire PM$_{2.5}$ contribution to the SC-CO$_2$.

