\section{Appendix: Data}

\subsection{Fire PM2.5 Source Data and Regridding}

\textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}, \textcite{park_attributing_2024}, and \textcite{zhao_global_2025} each generate global gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ datasets and project how climate change alters fire activity and associated concentrations, but differ in their temporal coverage, methodology, and treatment of non-climate drivers. All studies simulate gridded emissions estimates from fire, which are fed into chemical transport models to produce fire PM$_{2.5}$ contributions. The fire PM$_{2.5}$ contributions are isolated as the difference between simulations with and without fire emissions conducted under the same scenario. 

\textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} use Community Earth System Model (CESM)---a coupled chemistry-climate-land model---to simulate fire activity and PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations for a baseline period (2001--2010) and future periods (2041--2050 and 2091--2100) under RCP4.5 and RCP8.5. Their simulation design isolates the effect of climate change on fire activity by including both scenarios in which population density varies with SSP trajectories and scenarios in which it is held constant at year-2000 levels. In the chemical transport step, anthropogenic and biogenic emissions are likewise held constant within each snapshot period. We use only the population-constant simulations, as our regression targets the GMT--fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship net of demographic change (Section~\ref{sec:method_regression}).

\textcite{park_attributing_2024} simulate historical fire PM$_{2.5}$ from the 1960s through the 2010s using three fire--vegetation models (CLASSIC, SSiB4, and JULES) driven by historically observed climate forcings. For chemical transport, anthropogenic emissions are set to observed historical values for each decade from Community Emissions Data System Inventory (CEDS), and meteorological conditions in the chemical transport model are fixed at a single reference year, to attribute to climate change the variation in fire PM$_{2.5}$ across decades.\footnote{Specifically, \textcite{park_attributing_2024} fix GEOS-Chem meteorological conditions at 2016 values for all simulation periods (Table~1 of \textcite{park_attributing_2024}).} We use the factual simulations, which span six decadal means across three fire models, yielding 18 observations per country.

\textcite{zhao_global_2025} develop a machine learning model of burned area---trained on Global Fire Emissions Database version 4.1s (GFEDv4.1s) observed burned area, ERA5 climate reanalysis, and Earth system model (ESM) estimates---and driven by Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (CMIP6)-projected climate variables to produce global fire gridded emissions estimates for 2095--2099 under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5. These emissions are routed through the GEOS-Chem chemical transport model to produce fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations. Land-use inputs follow scenario-specific Land Use Harmonization 2 (LUH2) trajectories, so land use varies with the warming scenario. Anthropogenic emissions likewise vary with the SSP scenario, following CMIP6 protocols. 

We regrid PM$_{2.5}$ data from these multiple sources and native resolutions onto a unified $0.5^\circ \times 0.5^\circ$ global grid ($720 \times 360$), and subsequently merge the gridded fields with population data using longitude–latitude matching. The only difference across the three scripts lies in the resampling method: the \textcite{park_attributing_2024} dataset is interpolated using bilinear interpolation, the \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} dataset is remapped using nearest-neighbor matching, and the \textcite{zhao_global_2025} dataset is aggregated using area-weighted averaging. All other processing steps are identical, including the definition of the target grid, coordinate harmonization, and the final left join with population data based on exact longitude-latitude pairs. The resulting grid is defined at the center of each $0.5^\circ$ cell, with longitude spanning $-179.75^\circ$ to $179.75^\circ$ and latitude spanning $-89.75^\circ$ to $89.75^\circ$, at $0.5^\circ$ intervals.

\subsection{Global Mean Temperature Change}

Global mean temperature change values relative to pre-industrial times are associated with each PM$_{2.5}$ simulation. For the \textcite{park_attributing_2024} simulations, we assign historically observed GMT changes \autocite{owid_global_2026}. For \textcite{zhao_global_2025}, we assign GMT changes projected under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5 \autocite{anthoff_mimisspsjl_2026}. For \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}, we use their gridded GMT change data relative to 2005, to which we apply a pre-industrial offset \autocite{owid_global_2026}.  

\subsection{Appendix: Population Weighting}
\label{sec:app_pop_weight}

For grid cell $i$ in country $c$, let $PM_{itsm}$ denote fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) under time period $t$, scenario $s$, and fire model $m$. Each observation is already a period average: a decade mean for \textcite{park_attributing_2024} and \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}, and a five-year mean for \textcite{zhao_global_2025}.

Baseline population $\bar{pop}_{ic}$ is the average annual population in cell $i$ of country $c$ over 2001-2010, sourced from \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}. Population is held fixed at this baseline across all periods and scenarios to isolate changes in fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations. Country-level baseline population is
$\bar{pop}_c = \sum_i \bar{pop}_{ic}$.

Population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure for country $c$, period $t$, scenario $s$, and fire model $m$ is:
\begin{align}
\bar{PM}_{ctsm} = \frac{\sum_i PM_{itsm} \cdot \bar{pop}_{ic}}{\bar{pop}_c}, \nonumber
\end{align} 
which is the dependent variable in equation~\eqref{eq:regression}.

