\section{Appendix: \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} method}
\label{sec:app_qiu_method}

\subsection*{Summary of \textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} and \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}}

\textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} develop a fully data-driven modeling chain
linking climate change to wildfire smoke mortality in the USA. They (i) ``construct an ensemble of statistical and machine learning models that predict wildfire emissions as a function of climate and land use variables over North America''; (ii) ``use surface wildfire smoke PM2.5 estimates derived from satellite data and machine learning models to establish an empirical relationship between wildfire emissions and smoke PM2.5 concentrations across the contiguous USA at a 10-km resolution''; and (iii) use a Poisson distributed-lag model to estimate the effects of annual smoke PM$_{2.5}$ on county-level all-cause mortality rates from 2006-2019 \autocite[936]{qiu_wildfire_2025}. Future projections are generated by applying this empirical chain to bias-corrected climate projections from 28 CMIP6 GCMs under three SSP scenarios. \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} use this framework to estimate a quadratic damage function relating GMT change to smoke-related mortality in the USA, incorporating an empirical fire-fuel feedback that reduces projected fire emissions at higher warming levels, and incorporate the result into GIVE to compute a partial SC-CO$_2$.

\subsection*{Strengths and limitations of each approach}

\paragraph{Our approach.}
The principal strength of our approach is global coverage. Whereas
\textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} require 10-km resolution smoke PM$_{2.5}$ predictions for the USA, soil moisture from NLDAS-2---which is ``available only in the contiguous USA''---and county-level mortality data covering the contiguous United States, the process-based fire models we draw upon
\autocite{pierce_estimating_2017, park_attributing_2024, zhao_global_2025}
generate globally gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations that can be translated into country-level damage function parameters for all GIVE regions.

A further advantage is that process-based Earth System Models capture
physical mechanisms omitted by empirical models. \textcite[4]{pierce_estimating_2017} note that CLM4.5 ``uses a global dynamic vegetation model with carbon and nitrogen biogeochemistry\ldots to treat the response of vegetation to future climate and fires,'' thus incorporating how warming-driven fires can affect fuel load and future fire activity. \textcite{park_attributing_2024} likewise uses models incorporating similar physical mechanisms. In their machine learning approach, \textcite{zhao_global_2025} also incorporate a predicted biomass change ratio based on ESM-simulations.  \textcite[942]{qiu_wildfire_2025} acknowledge that their approach ``does not account for potential indirect effects of climate on wildfire through channels such as climate's influence on vegetation productivity or lightning-related ignitions.''  \textcite[5]{qiu_valuing_2026} partially address this by incorporating an empirical fire-fuel feedback---which alters future fire probability---but acknowledge that their estimates ``do not capture the effects of past fires on future fire severity, intensity, and shifts in vegetation composition or species, which may influence projected fire emissions, smoke, and mortality.'' Additionally, \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} isolate the impact of climate change through a simulation holding population density constant across scenarios, directly targeting the GMT-fire PM$_{2.5}$ relationship of interest.

The principal limitation of our approach is limited data. Whereas
\textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} estimate their damage function from 784 climate simulations (28 GCMs $\times$ 4 SSPs $\times$ 20 time periods), our country-level regression draws on 25 observations per country---sufficient to identify a linear slope but not curvature. Additionally, our uncertainty intervals are conditional: they reflect sampling uncertainty in $\beta_c$ and $\gamma$ but do not fully capture disagreement across fire models in the GMT-PM$_{2.5}$ slope, or uncertainty arising from alternative functional forms (Section \ref{sec:method_uncertainty}).

\paragraph{Qiu et al.}
A principal strength of \textcite[941]{qiu_wildfire_2025} is that they model ``the full causal chain linking climate to health through wildfires (climate change-wildfire–air pollution–mortality), and [they propagate] uncertainty from each step to understand uncertainty in likely health impacts.'' As they note, ``one advantage of our data-driven climate-fire model over process-based model projections is its ability to isolate the factors of climate variables from other factors that influence wildfires'' \autocite[941]{qiu_wildfire_2025}. In contrast, in the \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} model, land-use change assumptions (deforestation) influence future fire PM2.5 concetrations---a factor for which our regression does not control---but Pierce et al. also suggest that climate variables are the primary driver of concentrations \autocite{ford_future_2018}. Additionally, the Qiu et al fire-smoke model constrains smoke PM$_{2.5}$ directly from satellite observations, avoiding the reliance on chemical transport modeling assumptions that can yield estimates differing ``by an order of magnitude when compared with surface observations.'' Their approach also supports identification of a quadratic GMT-mortality relationship, and \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} incorporate an empirically estimated fire-fuel feedback that reduces the SC-CO$_2$ by 68\% relative to a no-feedback specification. This feedback effect is not explicitly captured in our regression but is accounted for in our PM2.5 simluation data, as noted above. 

The principal limitation of \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} is geographic scope. Their data requirements---particularly for satellite-derived smoke PM$_{2.5}$ at 10-km resolution and NLDAS-2 soil moisture---seem to confine the approach to North America, precluding global application. As noted above, an additional limitation is not fully capturing climate-vegetation dynamics or lightning ignitions, both of which process-based models represent explicitly \autocite{pierce_estimating_2017}.

\subsection*{Why \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} estimate stronger fire
PM$_{2.5}$ effects}

Several design choices in \textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} and
\textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} produce higher exposure-response per $\mu$g/m$^3$ and higher excess mortality estimates at higher warming levels.

\textit{Smoke-specific concentration-response function.}
\textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} empirically estimate a smoke-specific
exposure-response function, which they find to have a larger impact on all-cause mortality than all-source PM$_{2.5}$. Their results are larger than those used in prior work---including our application of \textcite{pope_fine_2020}: a 0.8\% increase in all-cause mortality per $\mu$g/m$^3$ of \emph{all-source} PM$_{2.5}$ exposure---because smoke PM$_{2.5}$ may have distinct chemical composition and toxicity. \textcite{qiu_wildfire_2025} state that this higher estimated exposure-response largely accounts for their higher mortality estimates.

\textit{Distributed-lag mortality.}
\textcite[940]{qiu_wildfire_2025} use a distributed-lag model and find that
``exposure to annual mean smoke concentrations of 0.75-1 $\mu$g/m$^3$
increases the mortality rate by 1.5\% in the same year, but by 2.7\% ...
if we also account for deaths occurring in the following year.''
\textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} adopt this approach, accounting for exposure in the current and prior year. Our approach applies a single-period all-source CRF and does
not capture this multi-year mortality effect.

\textit{Quadratic damage function.}
\textcite{qiu_valuing_2026} estimate a quadratic GMT-mortality relationship
from 784 simulations spanning 0.95-6.93$^\circ$C of warming.
As shown in our comparison (Table~\ref{tab:excess_deaths}), the quadratic
function exceeds our linear function in levels above approximately
3.4$^\circ$C, producing substantially more projected deaths at high warming
levels (e.g., 115,667 vs.\ 71,494 at 6$^\circ$C).

