Two features of Figure~\ref{fig:damage_functions} stand out. First, the dotted vertical line marks 1.7$^\circ$C---the GMT at which the \emph{marginal} excess death toll of the Qiu et al. quadratic first exceeds that of our linear function. Below this threshold, each additional degree of warming causes more \emph{marginal} deaths under our function than under \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}. Warming of $>$1.7$^\circ$C occurs over most years modeled in GIVE---from 2025 to 2300---driving higher SC-CO$_2$ results under the quadratic function. Second, the two curves cross in \emph{levels} at 3.4$^\circ$C, after which the Qiu et al. quadratic projects more excess deaths than our linear function for all higher warming levels.

GIVE outputs excess death results for each year, country, and iteration $n$.  Table~\ref{tab:excess_deaths} reports U.S.\ annual excess deaths averaged across all year-iteration pairs with GMT change falling within a $\pm 0.10^\circ$C window of each reported level. For example, the 1.5$^\circ$C row averages over all year-iteration pairs with $T \in [1.4, 1.6]$. These results confirm with model output the levels crossover shown in Figure~\ref{fig:damage_functions}. At 1.5$^\circ$C our function projects 12,681 excess deaths annually against 1,128 for \textcite{qiu_valuing_2026}, and at 2$^\circ$C the gap remains large (18,335 vs.\ 7,172). The estimates cross in levels between 3 and 4$^\circ$C (23,160 vs.\ 20,142 at 3$^\circ$C; 36,228 vs.\ 37,611 at 4$^\circ$C), after which the Qiu et al. function projects more excess deaths than our linear function at all higher warming levels (115,667 vs.\ 71,494 at 6$^\circ$C).

